%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
//What one Formative pass or Summative Test set out to establish and how: the Artifact and build evaluated, the ethics and consent basis for doing so, the Evaluation Method applied, and what was found. Formative and Summative reports share one template, distinguished by the Mode field.//

= Evaluation Report [ER-01] =

== Shared fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|ER-01
|Name|Cognitive walkthrough of UC04 with Mary and Myra
|Status|In Progress
|Mode|Formative
|Artifact used|AT-02, build Spec-1 (paper): the UC04 specification, DS-04, HP-05, HP-06 and RP-03, as they stood when the walkthrough was done.
|Evaluation Method(s) applied|(((
Ad hoc, not one of EM-05 to EM-07. A cognitive walkthrough is an inspection method with no end users involved. It addressed Claims, since it asks whether Mary and Myra can plausibly get the intended effect from the Functions. It also touched the Premises, but only for whether they are specified coherently: a paper build cannot verify timing or accuracy.

Variant: the baseline procedure from usabilitybok.org, with its four questions asked at each step. (Q1) Will the user try to achieve the right effect? (Q2) Will the user notice that the correct action is available? (Q3) Will the user associate the correct action with the effect they are trying to achieve? (Q4) If the correct action is performed, will the user see that progress is being made?
)))
|Ethics|No ethics review was required. The walkthrough involved no participants and no personal data: the evaluator worked from the specification and the Personas only.
|Introduction|(((
Addresses the Claims attached to the human-facing steps of UC04: UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL18, UC04-F8-CL19, UC04-F9-CL20, UC04-F7-CL21, UC04-F10-CL22 and UC04-F8-CL24. CL16, CL17, CL23 and CL25 depend on outcomes (prompt levels reached, felt autonomy, time saved, plaque) that a walkthrough cannot estimate, so they are left to ER-02.

Why now: the //Escalation specification// was added to answer the midterm feedback on how and when support escalates. A walkthrough is the cheapest check of whether that ladder makes sense to the people it is meant for, before a build is made. SCE supplies the walkthrough material: the Action Sequence, the Personas, and Premises and Claims tied to steps.
)))
|Method|(((
**What was done.** One evaluator walked UC04's Action Sequence twice: as Mary (HP-05) for the resident-facing steps, and as Myra (HP-06) for the handover and record steps. Steps that only the System performs (step 1, the start of tracking, and the inside of F7) were not walked, because nobody has to act on them. For each step that was walked, the evaluator tried to tell a credible story for each of the four questions, using what HP-05 and HP-06 say the person can and cannot do. Each failure story became a finding (WF-nn), with a design suggestion where one was obvious.

**Steps walked.** 2-alt (not started), 2 and 3 (arriving and starting), 4 to 8 at each SA-08 step from the //Ladder per step//, 3-alt (early stop during brushing), 8-alt (distress), the ladder rule on declining, 9 (handover, from both sides), 10 (safety path, Myra's side), and 11 (session record, Myra's side).

**Limits.** This is one pass by one evaluator. The procedure asks for 3 to 5 independent evaluators and a joint session, so the findings below are a first list, not an aggregate (O-39). The evaluator is not a person with dementia, and simulating one is the known weak point of the method; HP-05 is itself provisional. A walkthrough also says nothing about how often a problem would occur or how severe it would be for real residents.
)))

== Formative-only fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|Finding(s)|See //Walkthrough findings// below: a step-by-step table, then findings WF-01 to WF-12, grouped by the Claim they bear on.
|Implications|See //Implications// below. One finding, WF-07, was a clear specification defect and has been fixed in §2.3 (O-34). The others are recorded as §2.3 Open items O-35 to O-38 for a group decision before Build A. One case, WF-10, is added to the EM-05 pilot (O-29).

== Summative-only fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|Result(s)|not applicable (Mode is Formative)
|Discussion|not applicable (Mode is Formative)
|Conclusions|not applicable (Mode is Formative)

== Walkthrough findings ==

=== Step by step ===

Answers are Yes, Partly or No; a No or Partly points to the finding that explains it.

|=UC04 step|=Persona|=Q1 right effect|=Q2 notices action|=Q3 links action and effect|=Q4 sees progress|=Story
|2-alt Not started|Mary|Partly (WF-01)|Partly|Partly|Yes|At 10 minutes before the window closes, Pepper says "Good morning Mary, it is time for your teeth." If Mary is in the bathroom or near it, this is a credible success. If she is elsewhere in the room, she may not hear a voice from the bathroom doorway (O-30).
|2 and 3 Arrives, has not started|Mary|Yes|No (WF-01)|n/a|No|Mary stands at the sink and does not pick anything up. No item event happens, so no stall rule runs: step 4's T-stall starts only after the first lift, and the initiation path only acts at 10 minutes before the window closes. She can stand there unsupported for most of the window.
|4 Pick up toothbrush|Mary|Yes|Yes|Yes|Yes|She has lifted the cup and stalls. Pepper waits 15 s, then gives L1 and L2 ("Would you like to pick up your toothbrush?"). HP-05 says she can act on a short spoken cue, so this is a credible success. The silent wait fits HP-05's need for time before being prompted.
|5 and 6 Toothpaste|Mary|Yes|Partly (WF-04)|Yes|Partly|L2 is credible. If the blocker is opening the tube, L2 and L3 must show opening, which the merged step does not say. TECH-11 counts a lift and return as done, so she gets no sign if no paste reached the brush; the cost of that is small.
|7 Brush|Mary|Yes|Partly (WF-04)|Yes|Yes|L2 "You could brush your teeth now." is credible. At L3 the picture is on Pepper's chest tablet, at the doorway behind her, while she faces the sink and mirror. The L3 sentence repeats L2 and does not tell her to look at the screen.
|3-alt Early stop|Mary|Partly|Yes|Yes|Partly (WF-10)|"Would you like to brush a little longer?" is a single offer, with no call if she declines. Credible success. Unclear case: spitting or rinsing mid-brushing produces a tap or cup event, and the ladder does not say whether that is a pause in step 7 or the start of step 8.
|8 Rinse|Mary|Partly (WF-02)|Yes|No (WF-02)|Yes|L1 at this step is still "Mary, you are brushing your teeth this morning." After she has brushed, that can be heard as an instruction to brush again. If she picks up the brush, that is not the expected tap or cup event, so the ladder carries on as if she had not responded.
|9 Put away|Mary|Yes|Yes|Yes|Partly (WF-12)|L2 is credible. If the ladder runs out here nobody is called, which suits HV-14. The specification has no closing line; DS-04 has Pepper say "All done", the only point where Mary would hear that she has finished.
|Declining (ladder rule 3)|Mary|n/a|n/a|Partly (WF-03)|n/a|Every L2 is a yes/no question. Two quick "no"s, which a person may give before thinking about the question, end prompting with a routine call, reason "declined", even if she would have gone on.
|8-alt Distress|Mary|Yes|No (WF-05)|Partly|Partly|To get help she must say something the System recognises as asking for help, or touch Pepper's head or hand. Nothing tells her that touching the robot does anything, and the rule that F8 must not depend on speech recognition conflicts with recognising "help", "no" or "stop".
|9 Handover, resident side|Mary|n/a|n/a|n/a|No (WF-06)|Pepper says that someone is coming. Under H1 a routine call can wait 5 minutes for a re-send and 10 for a second care worker. Nothing is specified for Mary during that time, and she is not told when someone has answered.
|9 Handover, care-worker side|Myra|Yes|No (WF-07)|Partly (WF-08)|No (WF-08)|Myra is dressing another resident. The message gives the activity, step and reason, with a priority, but not which resident or which room. With several residents on the round (SA-10) she cannot tell where to go. The specification also times her acknowledgement without saying how she acknowledges.
|10 Safety path|Myra|Yes|No (WF-07)|Yes|Partly|An urgent message is distinct from a routine one and is re-sent every 60 s, which is credible. The missing room matters most here.
|11 Session record|Myra|Yes|Yes|No (WF-09)|n/a|The record says completed, highest level "demonstration", no call. Myra wants to know what support Mary needs (HP-06 task 3), but the record does not say at which step the demonstration was needed, so she cannot target her help next time.

=== Findings by Claim ===

|=ID|=Finding|=Suggestion|=Bears on
|WF-01|No stall rule covers a resident who has reached the sink but not lifted anything. The System cannot tell this apart from not having come in at all.|A presence signal at the sink that is not a camera, for example a floor or door sensor added to TECH-11, so that T-stall can start on arrival. Interacts with O-30.|UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL18
|WF-02|The L1 orienting sentence is the same at every step. At steps 8 and 9 it can be heard as an instruction to brush again, and at step 4 it describes brushing that has not started.|Make L1 step-aware while keeping it at the orienting level, for example "Mary, you have nearly finished your teeth" at steps 8 and 9.|UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL19
|WF-03|Phrasing every L2 as a yes/no offer makes "no" an easy answer, and two of them end prompting. Offers serve OBJ-14, so this is a trade-off with OBJ-10.|Count a "no" as declining only if no item event follows within the response window; keep L2 as an offer.|UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL19
|WF-04|At L3 the picture is behind the resident, and the spoken part does not point to it. The toothpaste step merges opening and applying, so the picture may show the wrong part.|Add a pointer to the L3 sentence ("There is a picture on my screen"), or show the picture where the resident is looking, depending on O-30. Give steps 5 and 6 their own L3 pictures.|UC04-F8-CL15
|WF-05|Ladder rules 3 and 4 need the System to recognise "no", "stop" and requests for help, which conflicts with the rule that F8 must not depend on speech recognition. The touch input in RP-03 cannot be discovered by the resident.|Recognise a small fixed set of words only (yes, no, stop, help), treat everything else as no reply, and verify the recognition rate in EM-05. Drop touch as a help input, or tell the resident about it.|UC04-F8-CL18, UC04-F8-PR8
|WF-06|After "someone is coming" the resident hears nothing more, for up to 10 minutes on a routine call.|Pepper says once more that someone is coming at the re-send time, and tells the resident when the care worker has acknowledged.|UC04-F8-CL18, UC04-F9-CL20
|WF-07|The handover message does not say which resident or which room.|Fixed: the room is now a fourth item of every handover message (O-34).|UC04-F9-CL20, UC04-F9-PR9
|WF-08|The handover rules time an acknowledgement, but no acknowledgement action is specified, and the resident is not told when one happens.|One-tap acknowledgement on the TECH-12 message, which also triggers WF-06's "Myra is on her way".|UC04-F9-CL20, UC04-F9-CL23
|WF-09|The three-item record does not say at which step the highest prompt level was needed, so it may not be enough for the care worker to adjust support. Adding the step would add detail about how a personal-care activity was performed.|Group decision between privacy (VT-08, HP-05's constraint) and usefulness at handover (HP-06).|UC04-F10-CL22
|WF-10|It is not specified whether a tap or cup event during brushing ends step 7 or is a pause inside it.|Add rinsing mid-brushing as a scripted case in the EM-05 pilot and settle the rule from it.|UC04-F8-CL24, UC04-F7-PR7
|WF-11|Prompts arrive just when the resident stops and name the step they are on, which shows that something follows what they do at the sink, even with Pepper's cameras covered.|Explain the item sensors when consent is sought, and in RP-03's framing. Whether residents remember this is unknown.|UC04-F7-CL21
|WF-12|Unexpected, outside the Claims: DS-04 ends with "All done", which the specification does not contain. Without it, the end of the routine gives the resident no sign of having finished.|Add a closing line to the prompt levels, in an ordinary adult register and without praise.|none (no Evidence)

== Implications ==

* **Applied.** WF-07: the room is added to every handover message in F9, the handover rules, UC04-F9-PR9, DS-04 and TDP-02 (§2.3 O-34). The walkthrough of steps 9 and 10 from Myra's side should be repeated on the revised text.
* **For a group decision before Build A.** WF-01, WF-02, WF-04, WF-06 and WF-12 change the resident-facing prompt script (O-35). WF-03 and WF-05 change how the System hears the resident (O-36). WF-08 adds an acknowledgement action to TECH-12 (O-37). WF-09 is a trade-off between the record's privacy and its usefulness (O-38).
* **For EM-05.** WF-05's word recognition and WF-10's rinse-or-pause rule become scripted cases in the first pilot (O-29).
* **For ER-02.** The walkthrough found ways UC04-F8-CL18's adverse effect could arise that do not depend on the wait W: standing unsupported at the sink (WF-01), missing the picture (WF-04), and waiting after a handover (WF-06). ER-02 therefore codes M-15 per step and records which ladder rule fired, so that a rise in distress can be traced to its step.
* **For the method.** Repeat the walkthrough with 3 to 5 independent evaluators, at least one with care experience if the group can find one, and compare their lists before merging them (O-39).

== Evidence produced by this report ==

* EV-01: walkthrough on independent step completion (UC04-F8-CL15)
* EV-02: walkthrough on frustration from waiting (UC04-F8-CL18)
* EV-03: walkthrough on infantilising prompts (UC04-F8-CL19)
* EV-04: walkthrough on completion after handover (UC04-F9-CL20)
* EV-05: walkthrough on the session record (UC04-F10-CL22)
* EV-06: walkthrough on the early-stop offer (UC04-F8-CL24)
* EV-07: walkthrough on feeling watched (UC04-F7-CL21)

