= Evaluation Report [ER-02] =

== Shared fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|ER-02
|Name|Within-resident baseline comparison of the tooth-brushing aid (study design)
|Status|In Progress (designed, not run)
|Mode|Summative
|Artifact used|AT-02, Build A (planned). The study may only start once EM-05 has verified UC04-F7-PR7 to UC04-F10-PR10 on that build. Any Premise that is only partly met has to be named in the Results of every Claim that rests on it.
|Evaluation Method(s) applied|EM-06 (Within-resident baseline comparison), addressing Claims only. EM-07 (care-staff interview) runs alongside on the same ward but is a separate report, because it has different participants and a different consent basis. UC04-F10-CL22, and the care-staff halves of CL19, CL21 and CL23, belong to that report.
|Ethics|(((
**Review.** Residents with dementia are vulnerable participants who mostly cannot give informed consent themselves. On the TU Delft HREC checklist that, and the collection of health data, rule out the light procedure: the group needs a full HREC application with a Data Management Plan. The study also adds a plaque examination to residents' care and changes their routine. The group has not yet checked whether that makes it research under the Dutch WMO (Medical Research Involving Human Subjects Act). If it does, an accredited medical research ethics committee (METC) has to review it as well, and research with adults who cannot consent is then allowed only under strict conditions. Asking the HREC this is the first step (O-40).

**Consent.** Written informed consent from each resident's legal representative, after an information meeting and a plain-language leaflet with pictures. On every study morning the care worker also checks the resident's own assent. Any sign of objection, in words or behaviour, means no observation that morning, and the resident can leave the study at any time without any effect on their care. Care staff give their own written consent for being observed (M-18) and interviewed (EM-07).

**Protections.** No video or audio recording, in line with HV-11 and RP-03. Observers code on paper, from the doorway. Data are pseudonymised, and health data are handled as special-category personal data under the GDPR. The brushing-time log (M-19 part b) exists only for the study and is deleted after analysis. Care workers' timing data (M-18) are not shared with the care home (ST-12) for any judgement of their work. Residents, representatives and staff are told that the robot is an automated system that follows a script. The plaque check is skipped if the resident is unwilling or distressed on the day.
)))
|Introduction|(((
Addresses the EM-06 Claims for the resident: UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL16, UC04-F8-CL17, UC04-F8-CL18, UC04-F8-CL19 (resident interview part), UC04-F9-CL20, UC04-F7-CL21 (resident interview part), UC04-F9-CL23 (observed time part), UC04-F8-CL24 and UC04-F9-CL25.

Why a formative check is not enough: ER-01 found where the design could fail, but a walkthrough cannot say how often a problem occurs or how bad it is for residents. Its evaluator also could not simulate a person with dementia reliably. Every Claim here is about an effect on residents or care workers in the real routine, so only observing that routine can support, qualify or challenge it.
)))
|Method|(((
**Participants.** Residents of one psychogeriatric ward in a Dutch care home, recruited through the care team and their legal representatives. Inclusion: a diagnosis of dementia; currently brushing their own teeth with verbal prompting from care staff (SA-08's existing workarounds); able to stand or sit at their own sink; own teeth or dentures. Exclusion: needs full physical help to brush; hearing that a hearing aid cannot correct to understand Pepper's speech; an oral condition for which the dentist advises against a plaque check; previous strong distress around robots or new devices. Target: 8 to 10 residents, so that at least 8 complete (see the analysis plan for why 8). This is close to the six residents in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008). Also the care workers on that ward's morning round, typically 4 to 8.

**Experimental design.** Within-subject single-case A-B-A-B design per resident, following the alternating baseline-intervention structure of COACH. A is usual care: the care worker prompts as they do now, and Pepper stands at the doorway switched to standby. B is the System: F7 to F10 are active, and the care worker continues the round and answers F9 messages. Starts are staggered by one week across residents, so that a change in all residents at the same calendar week can be told apart from the System's effect. One acclimatisation week comes first, with Pepper present and silent and nothing scored.

Keeping Pepper present in A means that A and B differ only in whether the Functions run, which is what the Claims are about. It also hides the phase from the plaque assessor (see Procedure).

Each phase lasts at least five scored mornings and until the resident's M-13 baseline shows no clear trend, planned as two weeks of weekday mornings (about 10 sessions). With acclimatisation, that is about nine weeks on the ward per resident.

**Variables.** Independent variable: condition, nominal with two levels (A usual care, B System), manipulated by phase. Grouping variable for UC04-F8-CL18, not manipulated: ability, coded per resident and step as lower-ability where the resident's baseline M-14 at that step is 3 (demonstration) or higher. Dependent variables are the Measures in //Hypotheses// below, with their measurement levels.

**Tasks.** The resident's own morning tooth brushing in their own bathroom, SA-08 steps 1 to 9, at their usual time. Nothing is added to the task.

**Measures.** M-13, M-14, M-15, M-16, M-17 (resident interview), M-18 and M-19, as defined on the §1.2 Measures page, plus two counts from the System log in B: which ladder rule ended each episode, and the F9 messages by type. M-16 is asked after sessions in both phases, because UC04-F8-CL17 compares it with baseline. M-17's three questions are about the robot and are asked after B sessions only.

**Procedure.** One study morning, in both phases unless marked:
~1. The care worker checks that the resident is well and assents. Otherwise there is no session that morning.
2. The care worker gives the usual reminder that it is time to brush, as at the start of DS-04.
3. A: the care worker prompts as usual. B: the System runs; the care worker goes on with the round and answers F9 messages.
4. One observer stands at the bathroom doorway next to Pepper and codes each SA-08 step live: unprompted completion (M-13), highest prompt level (M-14), and Resistiveness to Care behaviours with duration and intensity (M-15). In at least 20% of sessions a second observer codes independently. A third person time-samples the care worker's prompting episodes (M-18).
5. Within 10 minutes after the session, if the resident is willing and calm, the observer asks the three M-16 items, and in B also the three M-17 questions, taking no more than five minutes. Answers are written down, not recorded.
6. Twice a week, on fixed days, a trained assessor scores plaque (M-19 part a) within 30 minutes of brushing. The assessor is not told the phase and does not see the session; since Pepper is present in both phases, its presence does not reveal the phase.
7. Stopping rules: an H3 message, an observed M-15 behaviour at extreme intensity, or any objection ends the session and the care worker takes over. If two B sessions in a row end in distress, that resident leaves B, and the care team reviews W and the response windows, which is the fallback UC04-F8-CL18 names.

**Material.** AT-02 Build A: Pepper with the prompt script and tablet pictures, item sensors on the toothbrush, toothpaste and cup (TECH-11), the care worker's phone app with the handover message and session record (TECH-12). Paper coding sheets for M-13, M-14 and M-15, and an M-18 time-sampling sheet. A large-print card with the M-16 items and the M-17 questions. The MPS kit (two dental mirrors, a head torch) and its calibration manual (O-32). Leaflets and consent forms.
)))

