== Hypotheses and analysis plan ==

Each Claim becomes a yes/no question about the same residents in B compared with A. Unless the row says otherwise, the test uses one value per resident per phase type: the mean over that resident's A sessions and the mean over their B sessions. The confirmatory test is then a Wilcoxon signed-rank test across residents on those pairs, one-sided in the predicted direction, at α = .05. Holm's correction is applied across the six confirmatory tests marked with an asterisk. Effect sizes are reported with every test. Each resident's sessions are also plotted by phase, and judged for level, trend and overlap, as single-case designs are read.

|=Claim|=Question (yes/no)|=Dependent variable and level|=Analysis and decision rule
|UC04-F8-CL15 *|Do residents complete more SA-08 steps unprompted in B than in A, without more distress?|M-13, count of steps 0 to 9 per session (ratio). M-15, total 0 to 156 (treated as interval).|M-13: Wilcoxon, B greater than A. M-15 must not rise: the upper end of the confidence interval for the B minus A difference stays below 5 points, a margin the group has to confirm (O-40).
|UC04-F8-CL16 *|On steps where the resident stalls, is the highest prompt level lower in B than in A, with completion no worse?|M-14 per stalled step, 0 to 4 (ordinal). M-13 as above.|Per resident, the median M-14 over stalled steps; Wilcoxon, B lower than A. M-13 no worse, as in CL15.
|UC04-F8-CL17 *|Do observers rate support of autonomy higher in B, while residents rate their own autonomy no lower?|M-16 observer, 7-point (ordinal). M-16 self-report, mean of three 5-point items (ordinal, treated as interval).|Observer: Wilcoxon, B greater than A. Self-report: lower end of the confidence interval above −0.5 points, a margin to confirm (O-40). If the two disagree the Claim stays unresolved, as CL17 already states.
|UC04-F8-CL18|For lower-ability steps, does distress rise in B while unprompted completion does not improve?|M-15 and M-13, restricted to lower-ability steps; plus which ladder rule fired.|Too few residents are expected in this group for a test, so it is judged per resident from the plots. It holds for a resident if their M-15 on lower-ability steps is higher in both B phases than in both A phases, and their M-13 on those steps is not. The ladder log shows whether the rise follows the wait W or WF-01, WF-04 or WF-06 from ER-01.
|UC04-F8-CL19 (resident part)|Do residents say the robot talks down to them?|M-17 code T (nominal counts), from B interviews.|No test. Holds if any resident's answer is coded T, reported with the quote, or if M-15 during prompting rises above baseline (from CL15's data). Read together with EM-07.
|UC04-F9-CL20 *|Do fewer sessions end incomplete in B than in A?|Session outcome, complete or incomplete (nominal); per resident, the proportion incomplete (ratio).|Wilcoxon on the per-resident proportions, B lower than A.
|UC04-F7-CL21 (resident part)|Do residents say they feel watched?|M-17 code W (nominal counts), from B interviews.|No test. The Claim is supported for residents if no answer is coded W; every W is reported with its quote.
|UC04-F9-CL23 * (observed part)|Is the care worker's prompting time per session lower in B, with completion no worse?|M-18, seconds of prompting per session (ratio); notifications per round by type (count).|Wilcoxon, B lower than A. Completion no worse: the B minus A difference in the proportion incomplete stays below 10 percentage points, a margin to confirm (O-40). Notification counts are reported alongside, so that time saved on prompting is not just moved to messages (HFC-14).
|UC04-F8-CL24 * and UC04-F9-CL25|Do residents brush longer in B, and is there less plaque in B, or more?|M-19 part b, seconds of brushing per session (ratio), and the share of sessions reaching B-target. M-19 part a, Plaque Score 1 to 4 (ordinal).|Brushing time: Wilcoxon, B greater than A. Plaque: Wilcoxon on the per-resident mean Plaque Score, two-sided, because the two Claims predict opposite directions. Lower in B with longer brushing supports CL24. Higher in B means CL25 holds. Reported across residents only, since MPS was built for groups.

**Why 8 residents.** With Holm's correction over six tests, the smallest p-value must fall below .05 / 6 ≈ .008. A one-sided Wilcoxon test on n residents can reach at best p = 1/2^n, which is .016 for 6 residents and .004 for 8. So with fewer than 8 completing residents no confirmatory test could succeed, whatever the data. Even with 8, a test only succeeds if almost all residents move in the predicted direction. The single-case plots therefore carry as much weight as the tests, and a non-significant result is reported as "not shown", never as "no effect".

**Reliability.** Inter-observer agreement on the 20% double-coded sessions as each Measure's page states: Cohen's kappa for M-13, weighted kappa for M-14 and M-16 observer, ICC for M-15 and M-18 durations, with .60 as the minimum (Landis & Koch, 1977). Observers train on acclimatisation-week sessions until they reach it.

**Preregistration.** Hypotheses, margins, tests and stopping rules are registered (for example on OSF) before the first A phase. Anything decided after seeing data is reported as exploratory.

== Quality and logistics ==

|=Concern|=How this design deals with it|=What remains
|Reliability|Double coding of 20% of sessions, agreement thresholds per Measure, observer training before scoring.|M-14 and the tooth-brushing version of M-16 are untested scales.
|Validity|The real routine in the resident's own bathroom, with their usual care worker, so ecological validity is high. Pepper is present in both phases, so the comparison isolates the Functions.|The observer at the door is a presence the resident does not normally have, in both phases. M-19 part b measures time spent, not which teeth were cleaned.
|Bias|The plaque assessor does not know the phase. Return to A after B shows whether an effect lasts beyond the first exposure. Staggered starts separate the System from calendar events. Preregistration fixes the analysis in advance.|Observers cannot be blind, because Pepper speaks only in B. Novelty is reduced by the acclimatisation week, not removed.
|Scope|Inclusion criteria match HP-05.|One care home, one robot, residents who can still brush their own teeth with verbal cues. Results do not transfer to TECH-14 or to UC05.
|Budget and equipment|Pepper on loan, item sensors, a phone app, coding sheets, the MPS kit.|A dental hygienist's time for calibration and twice-weekly plaque checks needs funding.
|Time and location|About nine weeks per resident on one ward, after an EM-05 pilot, with staggered starts adding a few weeks.|Approval time for the HREC (and a METC, if needed) comes before all of this.

**Graph edges:** //uses → AT-02 (Build A, planned); applies → EM-06; produces → Evidence for the Claims listed in the Introduction, once run.//

== Evidence produced by this report ==

None yet: the study has not been run.
